      sources: "Sources: examples/non-person-identity fixtures (fictional). Chapter 12, “Lab 1: fixtures, no domain,” steps 2–3.",
      say: `Step 2 reads the findings. Look for the account called gmsa ops. It's in Domain Admins, SailPoint never aggregated it, and no request explains it: three findings for one account nobody asked for. Step 3 reads what the code hands to Chapter 6. Two rows, one per approved request, each carrying its SailPoint request and its ServiceNow change. The approved group managed account gets its retriever group, named for the payroll app. The delegated managed account gets no retriever entry, because it learns its hosts during its migration.`,
      detail: `Step 2, read the findings: Get-Content out/msa-standard/findings.json -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json | Sort-Object Rule | Format-Table Rule, Severity, ObjectKey. Look for gmsa-ops$: it's in Domain Admins (NPI-STD-16), SailPoint never aggregated it (NPI-STD-10), and no request explains it (NPI-STD-18). Step 3, read what the code hands to Chapter 06: Get-Content out/msa-standard/kerberos-replatform.csv; Get-Content out/msa-standard/password-retrievers.json. The approved gMSA gets its retriever group, and both rows carry the SailPoint request and the ServiceNow change. Expected: svc-billing to dmsa-billing$ (ScheduledTask, BILL01, end 2027-09-30, REQ-2026-0202, CHG0041202) and svc-payroll to gmsa-payroll$ (Service, PAY01;PAY02, payroll.owner@contoso.example, end 2028-12-31, REQ-2026-0201, CHG0041201), and a password-retrievers.json whose accounts has gmsa-payroll$ with CN=DL-gMSA-payroll-Hosts,OU=Groups,DC=corp,DC=contoso,DC=example. The dMSA has no entry: a dMSA learns its hosts during its migration.`,
