      sources: "Sources: Chapter 12, “Checklist”; “Continue the book”; the service account migration guide, “The managed service account standard.”",
      say: `Here's what done looks like. Every request was checked before the approvers saw it. Every row in Chapter 6's inputs came from an approved request, with its request number and change record. SailPoint aggregates every managed account, each with one owner, and no reconciliation finding is open without a ServiceNow finding and someone to fix it. Every certification is current, every revoke is a finding, and Defender for Identity shows no user account serving as a service account that isn't on a plan. Nothing was deleted except by a person, from the list. And each run's evidence and manifest are filed with the change record.`,
      detail: `Checklist: every managed service account request was checked by Chapter 12 before SailPoint's approvers saw it. Every row in Chapter 06's reviewed inputs came from an approved request, with its request ID and change record. SailPoint aggregates every gMSA and dMSA in <service-account-ou>, each with one correlated owner. No finding NPI-STD-10 to -19 is open without a ServiceNow finding and an owner of the fix. Every account's certification is current for its risk tier, and every revoke has a ServiceNow finding. Defender for Identity lists no user-type service account the register doesn't plan to convert. Nothing was deleted that wasn't a delete candidate on the retirement list, and a person deleted it through the decommission ticket. Each run's evidence.json and manifest.json are filed with the change record. Continue the book: previous, Chapter 11, Deploying to Test and Production; the workbook index; the managed service account standard.`,
